@@ -39,24 +39,24 @@ One term per thing. The supervisor, the subagents, and every brief use these ter
 
 | Term | Meaning |
 |---|---|
-| **Main workflow core** | The part of the main workflow every project reads whole, at every start, resume and calibration: the roles and the division of labour, the founding rules, the loop's shape, escalation and reporting, project state, and the standing rules that hold in every phase. It is not an extension point; no conditional workflow changes it. Today it is `supervisor.md` itself. |
-| **Phase module** | The procedure for one phase of the main workflow (intake, design, the interface phase, planning, run parameters, configuration, the delegation loop, the change cycle, reflection points and project end), read whole on entering its phase and at every calibration. Until a phase is moved into a file of its own under `workflow/phases/`, its section of `supervisor.md` is its phase module. |
-| **Extension point** | A named place in a phase module where a conditional workflow may change the procedure, and the only such place. Each is declared by its phase module and listed, with the kinds of change it accepts, in the extension-point registry (`workflow/extension-points.md`). |
+| **Main workflow core** | The part of the main workflow every project reads whole, at every start, resume and calibration: the roles and the division of labour, the founding rules, the loop's shape, escalation and reporting, project state, and the standing rules that hold in every phase. It is not an extension point; no conditional workflow changes it. Today it is the sections of `supervisor.md` other than its phase sections. |
+| **Phase module** | A procedure file of the main workflow, listed in the extension-point registry: intake, design, the interface phase, planning, run parameters, configuration, the delegation loop, the change cycle, reflection points and project end. A phase's value selects its module or modules (planning and building each take two), read whole on entering the phase and at every calibration. Until a module is moved into a file of its own under `workflow/phases/`, its section of `supervisor.md` is the module. |
+| **Extension point** | A named place in a phase module where a conditional workflow may add or set, declared by its module and listed with the kinds of change it accepts in the extension-point registry (`workflow/extension-points.md`). A replacement targets a whole module instead, and only one the registry marks replaceable. |
 | **Product type** | How a product reaches its user: where it runs, how it is delivered, and what its main interface is (a web app, a video game, a host extension). Each part has exactly one, fixed at intake, from the open list. |
-| **Sub-type** | A narrower product type within one product type (a Roblox game within video games; a Claude Code extension within host extensions), with a workflow of its own because it changes a phase, a gate, a check or the tooling. A part has at most one sub-type, of its own product type. |
+| **Sub-type** | A narrower kind within one product type (a Roblox game within video games; a Claude Code extension within host extensions), with a workflow of its own because it changes a phase, a gate, a check or the tooling. A part has at most one sub-type, of its own product type. |
 | **Trait** | A property that adds work whatever the product type (has screens, installable, spends money). A part may have any number, proposed at intake and settled at the design gate, from the open list. |
 | **Conditional workflow** | The collective name for a type workflow, a sub-workflow or a trait addition: a small file that changes the main workflow only at extension points; everything it does not mention is inherited. It can never remove a founding rule, weaken the merge gate, or skip the requirements register, the inventory or the reviews. |
 | **Type workflow** | The main workflow adapted to one product type: its changes at named extension points (`workflow/types/<type>.md`). It may add, replace and set. |
 | **Sub-workflow** | A more detailed version of a type workflow for one sub-type (`workflow/types/<type>/<sub-type>.md`): it inherits its type workflow and changes only what differs, at the same extension points, and may override its type workflow's replacement or setting. |
 | **Trait addition** | What one trait adds to the procedure (`workflow/traits/<trait>.md`), whatever the product type. A trait addition may only add, never replace or set, so any combination of traits composes. |
-| **Add** | A change that puts extra content at an extension point (questions, sections, diagram kinds, checks, brief constraints, stages, completion conditions, reflection items) beside what is already there. Additions from several conditional workflows apply together, in the order the core states. |
-| **Replace** | A change that substitutes a whole phase module, only where the phase is declared replaceable in the registry; only a type workflow or a sub-workflow may replace. |
-| **Set** | A change that fixes a value the main workflow leaves open at an extension point (the kind of performance budget, the delivery kind); only a type workflow or a sub-workflow may set. |
-| **Open list** | Every product type, sub-type and trait, each with its definition, default traits, workflow name and Written flag, against which projects are classified (`workflow/open-list.md`). Open to new entries by its own procedure; it also fixes the order trait additions apply in. |
+| **Add** | As a kind of change a conditional workflow makes: extra content at an extension point (questions, sections, diagram kinds, checks, brief constraints, stages, completion conditions, reflection items) beside what is already there. Additions from several conditional workflows apply together, in the order the core states. |
+| **Replace** | As a kind of change a conditional workflow makes: a whole phase module substituted, only where the registry marks the module replaceable; only a type workflow or a sub-workflow may replace. |
+| **Set** | As a kind of change a conditional workflow makes: a value the main workflow leaves open at an extension point fixed (the kind of performance budget, the delivery kind); only a type workflow or a sub-workflow may set. |
+| **Open list** | Every product type, sub-type and trait, against which projects are classified (`workflow/open-list.md`): each entry with its key, name, definition and Written flag; product types and sub-types also with their default traits and their workflow name or sub-workflow name. Open to new entries by its own procedure; it also fixes the order trait additions apply in. |
 | **Workflow name** | A product type's workflow named as the work rather than the product (`Host Extension Development`, `Game Development`), fixed in the open list and used in the status file's `Workflows:` line. |
-| **Sub-workflow name** | A sub-type's workflow named the same way (`Claude Code Extension`, `Roblox Game`), fixed in the open list and written after the workflow name and an arrow in the `Workflows:` line. |
+| **Sub-workflow name** | A sub-type's workflow named for the kind of product its sub-type makes (`Claude Code Extension`, `Roblox Game`), as the open list fixes it, and written after the workflow name and an arrow in the `Workflows:` line. |
 | **Workflow check** | `workflow-check.py`: the one-shot program that checks every conditional workflow against the open list and the extension-point registry, and a status file's `Workflows:` lines against the open list; `inventory-check.py` and `conformance-check.py` use it to judge a project's `Workflows:` line. An unwritten product type or sub-type fails; an unwritten trait is flagged and the work continues. |
-| **Compaction-survival list** | The short list a phase module opens with, naming what a session must re-read after a compaction or a resume before its next action in that phase; the session-start hook names the current phase's module and its list. |
+| **Compaction-survival list** | The short list a phase module opens with, naming what a session in that phase must re-read after a compaction or a resume before its next action. The phase modules written in release B each open with one. |
 
